WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Experienced language learners accept
uncertainty, except that they don't

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.380
fully understand and yet they keep going.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.240
Hi there Steve Kaufmann here today, and
today I wanna talk about comprehension.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:18.120
To understand more don't try to
understand too much and I'll explain,

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:23.040
but first of all, if you enjoy my,
uh, videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.330
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.260
And if you follow me on a
podcast, please leave a review.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.230
I do appreciate it.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.440
So to...

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:36.120
I mean, listening comprehension, reading
comprehension, comprehension to me is the

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.730
most important goal in language learning.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:42.720
If we understand well, we will
eventually speak well, eventually,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.300
as we have more opportunity to speak.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.210
If we don't understand very well, we can't
have meaningful conversations, we can't

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:55.920
enjoy movies, we can't enjoy books, we
can't do all of the things that are going

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.100
to improve our skills in the language.

00:01:00.019 --> 00:01:03.345
Of course, we all wanna speak well, but
we need to speak a lot in order to speak

00:01:03.345 --> 00:01:05.955
well, but that comprehension is so key.

00:01:05.955 --> 00:01:08.835
And I know a lot of people
struggle with comprehension.

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:14.835
So, um, I want to give you a hint,
a tip about comprehension, and that

00:01:14.835 --> 00:01:18.135
is don't try too hard to understand.

00:01:18.675 --> 00:01:22.545
In other words, accept the fact
that as you are progressing in the

00:01:22.545 --> 00:01:26.865
language, there are a lot of times
when you are not going to understand,

00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:28.875
and you still have to keep going.

00:01:29.295 --> 00:01:34.665
And don't try to nail down what it
is you're studying, say a lesson.

00:01:35.085 --> 00:01:41.145
Accept The fact that it's 60%, 40%
comprehension and move on to the next.

00:01:41.895 --> 00:01:45.675
Uh, and I, I often mention Manfred
Spitzer, the German neuroscientist,

00:01:45.675 --> 00:01:51.465
who said the brain needs repetition,
but the brain needs novelty.

00:01:51.735 --> 00:01:56.475
And I was talking to my brother who I
gave an iPad to and who's working on

00:01:56.475 --> 00:02:00.825
LingQ and he, he says, I always forget,
I study this sentence and I keep on

00:02:00.825 --> 00:02:05.535
forgetting and I thought to myself, yeah,
you're not supposed to try to totally

00:02:05.535 --> 00:02:07.245
understand and remember that sentence.

00:02:07.425 --> 00:02:11.805
You're supposed to just allow the
language to kinda enter your brain

00:02:11.925 --> 00:02:13.305
and let the brain get used to it.

00:02:14.385 --> 00:02:17.845
And so my brother said, it's
a bit like mowing a lawn.

00:02:18.465 --> 00:02:22.935
If the grass is high, then you have
to set the lawn mower at a higher

00:02:22.935 --> 00:02:27.935
level in order to have an easier
first pass through mowing the lawn.

00:02:28.265 --> 00:02:31.500
The second time through, you can
set the blades a little lower.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:38.040
If you try to force yourself to push
that lawnmower into very high grass,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.380
uh, you're not gonna get there.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.650
It's gonna be very hard work and
you won't, you won't be able to,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.280
to get the job done depending
on how long the grass is.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.590
Now, in theory, if you have to mow
the lawn twice, you would think

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.070
it's gonna take you twice as long.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.860
But if in fact there's just too much
resistance and you can't mold the lawn,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.540
you're not gonna get the job done.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:04.260
So it's far easier and I've had this
experience and I'll show you the picture

00:03:04.265 --> 00:03:09.690
of my lawn and my lawn mower and how I
adjust it and it's much easier for me,

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.080
particularly, we've had a lot of rain
I haven't been able to mold the lawn.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.880
And so when I go in there, I have a
first pass at a higher level, and then

00:03:17.885 --> 00:03:19.920
I have a second pass at a lower level.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.950
Every pass I pick up a little bit more.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:28.859
It's the same with the content
that I learn from, listen to, read.

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:32.070
First time I go through, I
don't fully understand it.

00:03:32.075 --> 00:03:33.899
I may listen a second time.

00:03:34.049 --> 00:03:39.030
I may move on to other lessons and
then I come back and even on something

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:43.799
as basic as the mini stories,
I always pick up something the

00:03:43.799 --> 00:03:45.870
second, third, fourth time through.

00:03:46.109 --> 00:03:48.810
So every time I mow that lawn, even...

00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:53.820
I'm, I'm flowing through it, I'm pushing
the mower through it easily, but I'm

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.859
getting a few more blades of grass.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.180
And on...

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:02.579
in that way, we will gradually enable
our brains to get used to the language.

00:04:02.579 --> 00:04:05.160
Don't force yourself to try to understand.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.750
If you can't understand, move on to
new material, bringing in new words,

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.845
bringing in new context, some of
which again, which you will understand

00:04:13.965 --> 00:04:15.584
some of which you won't understand.

00:04:15.975 --> 00:04:20.115
And I think it's very important that
we have that sort of an attitude I've

00:04:20.115 --> 00:04:26.094
said in other videos, uh, experienced
language learners accept uncertainty,

00:04:26.505 --> 00:04:30.015
accept that they don't fully
understand and yet they keep going.

00:04:30.825 --> 00:04:31.785
Push yourself forward.

00:04:31.785 --> 00:04:35.625
And then you can come back again for
a second pass with the lawn mower.

00:04:35.865 --> 00:04:40.065
But I think very often the, the
attempt to nail things down to fully

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:45.225
understand is a major obstacle to
comprehension, whether it be listening

00:04:45.225 --> 00:04:49.305
comprehension or reading comprehension,
and the two do reinforce each other

00:04:49.605 --> 00:04:51.885
as does the study of vocabulary.

00:04:52.035 --> 00:04:57.030
The goal of course is to understand
everything or as much as possible, but

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:03.750
before we get to that perfectly mown lawn,
uh, we have to make quite a few passes.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:08.820
And so we should be easy on ourselves
and not be too demanding, you know,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.950
not expect perfect comprehension.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:14.910
And if we keep moving forward,
we will eventually have better

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:16.950
comprehension understand more.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:21.390
And if we have good comprehension, we
will eventually have good speaking skills.

00:05:21.765 --> 00:05:23.535
So thank you for listening.

00:05:23.835 --> 00:05:26.325
And I have spoken before
about comprehension.

00:05:26.445 --> 00:05:30.375
So I'm gonna leave a couple of videos here
that I did earlier on the same subject.

00:05:31.235 --> 00:05:31.725
Bye for now.

